okay here's a question for you how do  you tell a story about telling a story  that was the creative challenge facing  the filmmakers of the 2015 Best Picture  winner spotlight a movie about an  investigative reporting team at the  Boston Globe who exposed systematic  child sex abuse by Catholic priests in  the Boston area the kind of storytelling  in question is journalism specifically  print journalism even more specifically  investigative print journalism what  director Tom McCarthy and his team  wanted to do was capture this kind of  storytelling as honestly as possible to  show what it really looks like when it's  done right once we settle on a project  we spend it your more investigating  investigative reporting can be a long  and tedious process full of dead ends  and monotonous tasks in other words it  can be boring that doesn't mean the  results are boring or that there aren't  moments of intensity in the reporting  only that the kind of journalism that  produces stories like the one in  spotlight involves a lot of mind-numbing  work in fact the ability to engage in  that kind of tedium is I think an  essential feature of investigative  journalism and that poses a problem for  anybody who might want to capture the  profession on film as it really is as  soon as there were movies there were  movies about reporters the first time  Charlie Chaplin ever appeared on-screen  in 1914 he played a swindler  impersonating a journalist in 1919 Harry  Houdini played a newspaper reporter who  is wrongly accused of murder and must  make a series of daring escapes in order  to find the real killer already in the  silent film era stereotypes start to  form of news people that are still  recognizable today of the intrepid  crusading journalist who like the  detective often uses questionable  tactics to land the big scoop or expose  the corruption  of the rich and powerful in these films  rights historian Howard good journalists  were by turns idealists and heart and  cynics Crusaders and midnight  conspirators our attitude toward them  continually swings from dark to bright  and back again this complicated  stereotype carried right into the  talkies the reporters in Howard Hawks  1940 classic his girl Friday embody all  these qualities perfectly they are  wisecracking and world weary sometimes  nasty and unscrupulous yet they fight  for the truth and the little guy against  the powers that be and the decades that  followed journalists on film were  sometimes heroes like Humphrey Bogart in  deadline USA and sometimes villains like  Kirk Douglas in ace in the hole or Burt  Lancaster and Tony Curtis in sweet smell  of success whether the reasons were good  or bad though reporters were always  willing to do whatever it took to get  the story for a brief time in the late  70s journalists became superheroes the  closest predecessor to spotlight is of  course Alan Peck EULA's all the  president's men about Bob Woodward and  Carl Bernstein peeling back the layers  of the Watergate scandal investigative  reporters were the perfect heroes for a  paranoid era shining the light of truth  into the dark corners of power with the  help of master cinematographer Gordon  Willis Pecola visualized this antagonism  by making the Washington Post newsroom a  place of bright light and deep focus  within a city  absolutely draped in shadow like  spotlight all the President's Men is  less about this story at the heart of  the film in this case Watergate and more  about the process the movie popularized  the phrase and though that's a great  line in practice following the money is  in all that exciting it involves a lot  of paperwork and cross-referencing  searching for people's contact  information dealing with bureaucrats  knocking on doors verifying and re  verifying that is the real unglamorous  daily slog of a report  and if you don't communicate that in a  movie about journalism you haven't  really done what you set out to do you  haven't really depicted journalism but  you can't make a movie boring just so  that it's authentic  the challenge for Pak Allah and 40 years  later for Tom McCarthy is to dramatize  the undramatic one way to do that is by  using montage  even though montages can be cliche they  are still a useful tool for conveying  effort over long periods in spotlight a  key development in the investigation  occurs when reporters discover pedophile  priests are being classified as being on  sick leave in church directories so they  scrub years and years of these  directories to identify priests with  that designation tracking what they find  in a spreadsheet now there's nothing  exciting about inputting data into Excel  but a montage allows McCarthy to show  this crucial part of the investigation  in a lively way while also conveying the  feeling of drudgery it required another  tool and by definition a less  appreciated one is understatement  spotlight uses this even more than all  the president's men the whole film is d  stylized the cinematography is simple  the color palette is muted the  production and costume design messy and  modest Howard Shores score isn't  in-your-face and it's used sparingly  McCarthy's Direction doesn't call  attention to itself his camera moves are  functional inconspicuous now this isn't  a knock on the people who worked on  these things the opposite in fact it  takes just as much art to create a  reserved aesthetic as it does to create  a stylized one it's going to get a lot  less attention but that's the point the  understated tone of spotlight from the  performances to the visuals to the music  serves the story perfectly it tells us  something vital about the nature of good  journalism that the work is sober and  its practitioners meticulous that some  deeply important truths only exist on  the other side of months or years of  unglamorous reporting it's a hard tone  to get exactly right a few years after  spotlight Spielberg made the post which  has the same goals but I think that  leans a little too far  into righteousness to the point where  this newsroom aesthetic became  susceptible to parity newspaper movie  featuring men in bad ties and  short-sleeved colored shirts gross  styrofoam coffee cups and you can bet  your sweet ass there's a scene with a  very long pregnant pause while everyone  waits for the editor to say let's let's  go let's let's do it let's let's let's  go let's go let's go let's let's publish  like anything else it's important to  capture journalism honestly when we see  what reporting really looks like we  better understand what resources are  required for the best version of it to  thrive spotlight shows us what the gold  standard is to quote David Simon crater  of the wire it's a bunch of people who  go out and acquire information in a  systemic way and then bring it back to a  collective of people with real  experience and real institutional memory  who have an understanding of the  continuity and context of issues and can  determine the news value and publish it  accordingly and getting that complex and  nuanced message through in a two-hour  movie is impressive then again  journalists do that kind of thing all  the time  hey everybody thank you so much for  watching this episode was brought to you  by Amazon Studios and the Amazon  original film the report starring Adam  driver and net Bening and Jon Hamm thank  you to Amazon Studios for sponsoring  this video and agreeing like all my  other sponsors to let me have full  creative control on this piece Amazon  actually sent me the report a couple  months ago and I really liked it it's  powerful work from writer director Scott  Z burns and everybody involved and  though it's not a movie about journalism  it has a lot of the same thriller and  investigative elements as all the  President's Men and spotlight critics  have called the movie gripping and  eye-opening a portrait of American  heroism and festival crowds where it's  played seem to really like i